:step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157310 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157114 :question/id #uuid "ae98c2fc-bfb5-5f5d-bfce-f536aa42e5d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7496"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437139}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "4b44c854-917d-5ba0-b5a2-d41308ca5256" :step/math-academy-id 27840 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157115 :question/id #uuid "5be23338-73b5-50b5-8dc8-0aa6d264afb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7497"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437138}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "61e344b0-9976-54f7-a03c-175fe1505fbb" :step/math-academy-id 27841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158491 :tutorial/content "A **Poisson distribution** is a probability distribution that models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nIf the rate of the events is $\\lambda,$ then the number of events $x$ occurring in an interval of fixed length has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,3, \\ldots\n$$\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a Poisson distribution with rate $\\lambda,$ then we write $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda).$\n\nFor example, suppose that calls come in at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute at a call center. Then the number of calls $X$ that are received during the next minute has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the probability of receiving $4$ calls during the next minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{4}{e}^{-3}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.168\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "6e07ef85-f3ca-52c8-89e2-062d8ea7c9eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4525 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fd4dd871-25f7-54f5-9345-a556f2356cb0" :step/math-academy-id 27850 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158170}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158493 :tutorial/content "The shape of the graph of the Poisson distribution depends on the value of $\\lambda.$ When $\\lambda$ is small, the probability is concentrated at small values of $x.$ When $\\lambda$ is larger, the probability is concentrated at larger values of $x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8af371733bd248a4e5ba3bc35199f00414188e9ca033a04dee2a7de4b0a7db4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f0026198-16d4-5332-9f5b-036bd00ef32d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4534 :tutorial/title "The Graph of the Poisson Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "3576e13c-4960-5e5a-b072-2acaff8161cf" :step/math-academy-id 27932 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158155}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Poisson Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157493}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437141} :activity/id #uuid "6e3ee6dd-60a3-557f-8bd4-cac744c68b82" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048934 :topic/id #uuid "cd3b7077-78e5-5eb6-880e-d8648790c6b0" :topic/math-academy-id 2838} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd87632-86c9-568a-93e5-d7ef69437ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.827" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437116} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5cd1e1-b1c5-5fc7-9253-2dc3e4f28a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.979" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437118} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8348b8-c42d-56b0-b92b-798242f8369b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.865" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437122} {:answer/id #uuid "b61aade4-803e-5239-8e68-f83d9c20a8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.798" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437126} {:answer/id #uuid "fe83b2b1-31dd-59c3-9890-738677753877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.812" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437116} :answer-field/id #uuid "80be0045-2641-54de-af08-8fe8e34ad2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "630f14f8-eb57-54c4-9fbf-4988a90b57a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-85394" :question/problem "A telephone exchange receives an average of $7$ calls every $5$ minutes. What is the probability that the exchange will receive at least $5$ calls in the next $5$ minutes? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that calls occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of calls received in the telephone exchange. Since the average rate is $7$ calls every $5$ minutes, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of calls can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 5),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=1-P(X<5)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}{e}^{-7}}{0!}+\\frac{{7}^{1}{e}^{-7}}{1!}+\\frac{{7}^{2}{e}^{-7}}{2!}+\\frac{{7}^{3}{e}^{-7}}{3!}+\\frac{{7}^{4}{e}^{-7}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.173\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of receiving at least $5$ calls in the next $5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.173 \\\\  & =0.827\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "004acfbe-1d4d-5f2f-b440-224bff7b1696" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437144}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "993a5061-81f4-5185-bc75-a6608906c4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156046} {:answer/id #uuid "bb85757a-c98d-5e3e-84d6-7a62598ca493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156047} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9d3125-b7f6-515d-b49c-71622de1db1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.147" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156048} {:answer/id #uuid "07ca3f4d-0088-5e6b-b659-17cf0e9f2ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.173" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156049} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee4152b-ecde-55d6-b908-b244e85fc5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156048} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e3d202-ddbe-575d-aec9-41da45239dbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0cb030d4-b2c6-50c8-943d-58caad6b6e01" :question/math-academy-id "q-85150" :question/problem "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, there have been $4$ typos per page. What is the probability of finding $2$ typos on the next page? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that typos occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of typos on a page of the book. Since the average rate is $4$ typos per page, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of typos can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(4).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x}{e}^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding $2$ typos on the next page is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{2}{e}^{-4}}{2!} \\\\  & ≈0.147\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "212ee944-3799-52f3-82f8-1c83bc934474" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437140}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437142 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9e78be-1c05-5746-af99-56ee649b8e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.151" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437112} {:answer/id #uuid "23e97ddf-d3f7-50f6-875a-5463203ee997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.221" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437115} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff4f1e3-cf6e-5cd8-8f0c-fc33cf9a7534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437120} {:answer/id #uuid "99acdea4-f1d6-5ba9-80fa-995cba142c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.253" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437123} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8c3282-52ef-59d2-8559-f4a7b0b33e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437112} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa4c72e-18f1-5153-bc4d-2cd8d8207293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57e8cbd7-ad98-573e-bd99-c98a3e1a7b22" :question/math-academy-id "q-131276" :question/problem "The mean number of bacteria per milliliter of a liquid is $6.$ What is the probability that there will be no more than $3$ bacteria in one milliliter of the liquid? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that bacteria appear independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of bacteria in one milliliter of the liquid. Since the average rate is $6$ bacteria per milliliter, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of bacteria can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =6,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(6).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{6}^{x}{e}^{-6}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that at most $3$ bacteria will be found in one milliliter of the liquid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{0}{e}^{-6}}{0!}+\\frac{{6}^{1}{e}^{-6}}{1!}+\\frac{{6}^{2}{e}^{-6}}{2!}+\\frac{{6}^{3}{e}^{-6}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.151\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "a6a9e584-e23b-5f27-aa56-050dc0d31fff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437143}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437143 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16527557-36e3-5765-85fe-85ad503bd10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.907" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437114} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7ef5fa-25a0-50e1-8283-427fee96d7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.890" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437117} {:answer/id #uuid "9b51b374-afa5-5992-b9cf-b6d3ff5216f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.904" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437124} {:answer/id #uuid "c22b8e86-37a9-5abd-9b15-d5deb571c1ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.881" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437127} {:answer/id #uuid "ed10d1fe-7030-5542-b1f6-229aafd2780f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.981" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437129} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fbd0a6e-1d11-5207-93f7-e444bfce1656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c5edc979-6530-503b-9c43-a29e848f0191" :question/math-academy-id "q-131281" :question/problem "Previous results indicate that, on average, for each batch of transistors, $1$ is defective. What is the probability that in the next batch of transistors, there will be less than $4$ defective transistors? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that defective transistors occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of defective transistors in a batch of transistors. Since the average rate is $1$ defective transistors per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of defective transistors can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =1,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(1).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{1}^{x}{e}^{-1}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that in the next batch of transistors, there will be less than $4$ defective transistors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{0}{e}^{-1}}{0!}+\\frac{{1}^{1}{e}^{-1}}{1!}+\\frac{{1}^{2}{e}^{-1}}{2!}+\\frac{{1}^{3}{e}^{-1}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.981\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "db7e6473-de0a-51cd-a4c0-7c7adca3341e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "103af267-4448-5f1d-b2c7-5d3a6eb5caa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437113} {:answer/id #uuid "5546866d-cf0c-5f34-a20b-0d37698b92d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437119} {:answer/id #uuid "7a864749-ecf3-5067-80f4-653b14e5fe85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437121} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc7c554-abbe-5a01-96eb-fd4a8610edf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437125} {:answer/id #uuid "d4920049-b97d-5e34-bd87-a602c7c19c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437119} :answer-field/id #uuid "c35bc065-fcec-52cb-96c4-b81be5324354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "033d05af-1912-5a6e-86f3-f9dda400af0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131102" :question/problem "A company rents cars to tourists. On average, there have been $3$ rental requests each day. What is the probability that none of the cars are rented the next day? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that rental requests occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cars rented in a day. Since the average rate is $3$ requests per day, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of rented cars can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(3).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}{e}^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that none of the cars are rented the next day is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0) & =f(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{0}{e}^{-3}}{0!} \\\\  & ≈0.050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "f54fa653-ea98-5ece-9516-abf27f41ad60" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437142}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Poisson Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437111}]}
